  "Number": 13,
  "Question": "How many zeros are at the end of the product \\[ \\begin{aligned} &25 \\times 25 \\times 25 \\times 25 \\\\ &\\quad {}\\times 25 \\times 25 \\times 25 \\\\ &\\quad {}\\times 8 \\times 8 \\times 8? \\end{aligned} \\]",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(3\\)",
    "B": "\\(6\\)",
    "C": "\\(9\\)",
    "D": "\\(10\\)",
    "E": "\\(12\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Multiplying by \\(10\\) appends a zero, and each factor of \\(10\\) can be made by pairing a \\(2\\) with a \\(5\\).\n\nEach of the seven factors of \\(25\\) supplies two factors of \\(5\\), giving \\(14\\) altogether. Each of the three factors of \\(8\\) supplies three factors of \\(2\\), giving \\(9\\) altogether.\n\nWe can therefore form nine pairs, leaving five factors of \\(5\\). Thus\n\\[\n\\text{product}=5^5\\times10^9=3125\\times10^9.\n\\]\nSince \\(3125\\) does not end in zero, the product ends in exactly \\(9\\) zeros, choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
